      q_en: "How are glycogen breakdown and synthesis regulated, and how does the liver differ from muscle?",
      q_cn: "糖原的分解与合成是怎样被调控的？肝与肌肉有什么不同？",
      model_en: "There are two layers, covalent and allosteric, and they run at the same time. The covalent layer is a cascade. Epinephrine on muscle or glucagon on liver activates a G protein, adenylyl cyclase makes cyclic AMP, and cyclic AMP activates protein kinase A. Now the important asymmetry: protein kinase A phosphorylates glycogen synthase directly and switches it off, but it does not touch phosphorylase. What it phosphorylates instead is phosphorylase kinase, and that enzyme puts a phosphate on serine fourteen of each of the two subunits of phosphorylase b, turning it into phosphorylase a and driving a T to R transition. So one signal, two opposite effects, and the two arms are of different lengths. The reason is that phosphorylase has exactly one kinase while synthase has several, GSK3 primed by casein kinase two, AMP-activated kinase, and protein kinase A itself. The extra tier buys amplification: Lehninger's figures run from x molecules of hormone through twenty times cyclic AMP, ten times active kinase A, a hundred times phosphorylase kinase, a thousand times phosphorylase, to ten thousand times product, and it calls those low estimates. The allosteric layer is superimposed on that and it is tissue-specific. In muscle, calcium, the same signal that triggers contraction, activates phosphorylase kinase through its calmodulin delta subunit, so every ordinary contraction mobilises glycogen with no hormone at all; and AMP from ATP breakdown activates phosphorylase directly, while ATP blocks the same site. In liver, glucose binds an inhibitory site on phosphorylase a and exposes its phosphoserines to phosphatase one, so liver phosphorylase is its own glucose sensor; and on the other side glucose six phosphate makes glycogen synthase a better substrate for the same phosphatase. That one phosphatase, PP1, reverses all three enzymes and is delivered to the granule by targeting subunits, GM in muscle and GL in liver. Insulin works here, activating PP1 and inactivating GSK3 by having PKB create a pseudosubstrate out of GSK3's own amino terminus. As for liver versus muscle: muscle uses its glycogen only for itself, swings enormously in ATP demand, and cannot do gluconeogenesis. So myocytes have no glucagon receptors, the muscle pyruvate kinase is not phosphorylated by PKA so cyclic AMP actually speeds muscle glycolysis instead of stopping it, insulin recruits GLUT4 from vesicles in muscle while liver GLUT2 is always present, and only liver and kidney have glucose six phosphatase, so only they raise blood glucose. McArdle disease is the muscle phosphorylase and Hers disease the liver one, which is the isozyme argument made by nature.",
